ggplot(distances_window, aes(x = year)) +
  geom_line(aes(y = congiurati, color = "Intra-Grandi Curti")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = congiurati_vs_non, color = "Extra-Grandi Curti"), linetype = "dotdash") +
  geom_line(aes(y = longhi, color = "Intra-Longhi")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = longhi_vs_non, color = "Extra-Longhi"), linetype = "dotdash") +
  geom_line(aes(y = longhi_vs_congiurati, color = "Inter Longhi-Grandi Curti")) +
  labs(x = "Year",
       y = "Average Shortest Path Length") +
  scale_color_manual(
    name = NULL,
    breaks = c("Intra-Grandi Curti", "Extra-Grandi Curti",
               "Intra-Longhi", "Inter Longhi-Grandi Curti", "Extra-Longhi"),
    values = c("Intra-Grandi Curti" = "red", "Extra-Grandi Curti" = "red",
               "Intra-Longhi" = "gold", "Extra-Longhi" = "gold",
               "Inter Longhi-Grandi Curti" = "goldenrod"),
    guide = guide_legend(
      override.aes = list(
        linetype = c("solid", "dotdash", "solid", "solid", "dotdash")
      )
    )
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

# Some minima
min_ASPL_longhi <- year_at_min(distances_window,"longhi")
min_ASPL_extra_longhi <- year_at_min(distances_window, "longhi_vs_non")

# Burt's components over time
component_summary <- components_over_time %>%
  group_by(year) %>%
  summarise(mean_direct = mean(direct, na.rm = TRUE),
            mean_indirect = mean(indirect, na.rm = TRUE))

# 1. Simple Filtering: If you just want to isolate all rows where this is true
greater_values <- distances_window %>%
  filter(congiurati > longhi_vs_congiurati)

# 2. Interval Bounds: To find the continuous blocks (start and end indices)
# Create a logical vector for the condition
condition <- distances_window$congiurati > distances_window$longhi_vs_congiurati

# Replace any potential NAs with FALSE so rle() doesn't break
condition[is.na(condition)] <- FALSE

# Apply run-length encoding to find continuous sequences of TRUE/FALSE
runs <- rle(condition)

# Calculate the start and end row indices for every run
end_idx <- cumsum(runs$lengths)
start_idx <- end_idx - runs$lengths + 1

# Extract only the intervals where the condition evaluated to TRUE
interval_bounds <- data.frame(
  start_row = start_idx[runs$values == TRUE],
  end_row = end_idx[runs$values == TRUE]
)

# If your dataframe has a date or time column (e.g., 'year'), you can map these indices back:
# interval_bounds$start_time <- distances_window$year[interval_bounds$start_row]
# interval_bounds$end_time <- distances_window$year[interval_bounds$end_row]

first_period <- paste(
  distances_window$year[interval_bounds$start_row[2]], 
  distances_window$year[interval_bounds$end_row[2]], 
  sep = "-"
)

# Extract the very last interval block
last_idx <- nrow(interval_bounds)
last_period <- paste(
  distances_window$year[interval_bounds$start_row[last_idx]], 
  distances_window$year[interval_bounds$end_row[last_idx]], 
  sep = "-"
)
@

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.analysis}, the intra-faction ASPL for both factions is consistently lower than the extra-faction ASPL, the latter being approximately twice as large throughout the period. This indicates that families belonging to the same faction were, on average, more closely connected to one another than to the rest of the patriciate. At the same time, the \emph{longhi} generally display slightly lower intra-faction ASPL than the \emph{grandi curti}, suggesting stronger internal cohesion, while the inter-faction ASPL remains only slightly larger than the two intra-faction ASPLs through most of the period, indicating that marriages between the two elite factions were also frequent.

A second feature of the figure is the common temporal pattern shared by all five series. Average shortest path lengths decline until approximately the second half of the sixteenth century, indicating increasing cohesion within the marriage network. Thereafter, all measures begin to rise, reflecting a gradual reduction in overall connectedness. This reversal is more pronounced for the \emph{grandi curti}, whose internal cohesion begins to weaken earlier and more rapidly than that of the \emph{longhi}.

Third, the extra-faction ASPL suggests a gradual change in the relative position of the two factions within the broader network. During the first part of the period, the \emph{grandi curti} maintain a slight advantage, being on average closer to families outside their own faction. This advantage disappears towards the end of the sixteenth century, when the \emph{longhi} become marginally better connected to the rest of the network. 
A more striking change occurs around \Sexpr{first_period}, when the inter-faction ASPL falls below the intra-faction ASPL of the \emph{grandi curti}. After an inversion in the next period, during \Sexpr{last_period} a \emph{grandi curti} family is, on average, closer to a \emph{longhi} family than to the families in their own faction. This crossover points to a profound reorganization of the marriage network, suggesting that the structural separation between the two elite factions had largely disappeared.

These results suggest that the \emph{grandi curti} initially occupied a structurally advantageous position within the marriage network, a position that may have facilitated the formation of the broad coalitions required to sustain their long-standing monopoly over the dogeship. They also point to a gradual weakening of this advantage around the turn of the seventeenth century through changes that could help explain the end of that monopoly. 

Since the ASPL captures only one aspect of network structure, however, these conclusions remain tentative. A complementary perspective is provided by normalized closeness centrality \citep{freeman1979,wasserman1994} which measures the overall proximity of each family to the rest of the marriage network. We first compare the average closeness of the two factions and then examine the distribution of closeness values within each group. Unlike the intra- and extra-faction ASPL, which separately measure proximity within the faction and to the rest of the network, average closeness centrality summarizes both dimensions by providing a single measure of a faction's overall proximity to the network.

<<casenuove.closeness, warning=FALSE, echo=F, message=F, fig.cap="Evolution of closeness centrality over time.", fig.height=3, fig.pos="h!tb">>=
ggplot( network_measures_year, aes(x = year)) +
  geom_line(aes(y = closeness_congiurati, color = "Grandi Curti")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = closeness_longhi, color = "Longhi")) +
  labs(x = "Year",
       y = "Average closeness centrality") +
  scale_color_manual(name=NULL,
                     values = c("Grandi Curti" = "red", "Longhi" = "gold")) +
  theme_minimal()

# Check correlation
# Split at the crossover (~1550, based on your chart)
post_crossover <- network_measures_year %>% filter(year >= 1550)

# 1. Raw correlation on levels (can be inflated by shared trend)
cor_levels <- cor.test(post_crossover$closeness_congiurati, post_crossover$closeness_longhi)

# 2. Correlation on year-over-year changes (removes shared trend, tests real co-movement)
post_crossover <- post_crossover %>%
  arrange(year) %>%
  mutate(
    delta_congiurati = closeness_congiurati - lag(closeness_congiurati),
    delta_longhi      = closeness_longhi - lag(closeness_longhi)
  )

cor_deltas_post <- cor.test(post_crossover$delta_congiurati, post_crossover$delta_longhi)


# 3. For comparison: same two tests on the pre-crossover period
pre_crossover <- network_measures_year %>% filter(year < 1550)
cor_levels_pre <- cor.test(pre_crossover$closeness_congiurati, pre_crossover$closeness_longhi)

pre_crossover <- pre_crossover %>%
  arrange(year) %>%
  mutate(
    delta_congiurati = closeness_congiurati - lag(closeness_congiurati),
    delta_longhi      = closeness_longhi - lag(closeness_longhi)
  )

cor_deltas_pre <- cor.test(pre_crossover$delta_congiurati, pre_crossover$delta_longhi)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.closeness} reports the average closeness centrality of the two dominant factions, the \emph{grandi curti} (red line) and the \emph{longhi} (gold line). Values remain relatively high throughout the period: An average closeness of about 0.5 implies that, on average, any family could reach another in approximately two steps within the main connected component, which, as shown in Figure~~\ref{fig:casenuove.cohesiveness.setup}, contains virtually all families throughout the period.

The figure again reveals a marked change around the middle of the sixteenth century. Until approximately 1550, the \emph{grandi curti} exhibit consistently higher closeness centrality than the \emph{longhi}, allowing them to reach the rest of the patriciate through shorter paths. This advantage disappears in 1550, after a sharp decline in the closeness of the \emph{grandi curti}. From that point onward, closeness shows an overall increasing trend for both factions, with the \emph{longhi} maintaining a slight advantage until the two series converge towards the end of the period.

Another interesting pattern emerges after the crossover. The evolution of closeness centrality becomes much more synchronized across the two factions. The correlation between changes in the two series rises from \Sexpr{round(cor_deltas_pre$estimate,2)} before the crossover to \Sexpr{round(cor_deltas_post$estimate,2)} thereafter, with the corresponding p-values equal to \Sexpr{round(cor_deltas_pre$p.value,3)} and \Sexpr{round(cor_deltas_post$p.value,3)}, respectively.
 The stronger and more statistically robust correlation after the crossover is consistent with a structural change in the network, with the two factions evolving in a more coordinated manner from that point onward.

<<casenuove.closeness.histo, echo=F, message=F, fig.cap="Evolution of closeness centrality over time", fig.height=5, fig.pos="h!tb">>=
years_of_interest <- c(1500, 1575, 1625)

closeness_snapshots <- data.frame(
  family = character(),
  closeness = numeric(),
  betweenness = numeric(),
  Faction = character(),
  year = integer()
)

for (last_year in years_of_interest) {
  y <- last_year - DEPTH_IN_YEARS

  marriages_window <- noble_marriages_filtered %>%
    filter(year <= last_year & year >= y)

  marriage_graph <- graph_from_data_frame(
    marriages_window,
    directed = FALSE,
    vertices = unique(c(marriages_window$husband_familyname_std, marriages_window$wife_familyname_std))
  )

  E(marriage_graph)$weight <- 1
  marriage_graph <- simplify(marriage_graph, edge.attr.comb = "sum")

  V(marriage_graph)$Group <- ifelse(V(marriage_graph)$name %in% longhi, "Longhi",
                              ifelse(V(marriage_graph)$name %in% curti_congiurati, "Grandi Curti", "Curti"))

  component <- components(marriage_graph)$membership
  main_id <- which.max(table(component))
  marriage_graph_main <- induced_subgraph(marriage_graph, which(component == main_id))

  closeness_scores <- closeness(marriage_graph_main, normalized = TRUE)
  betweenness_scores <- betweenness(marriage_graph_main)

  closeness_snapshots <- rbind(closeness_snapshots, data.frame(
    family = V(marriage_graph_main)$name,
    closeness = closeness_scores,
    betweenness = betweenness_scores,
    Faction = V(marriage_graph_main)$Group,
    year = last_year
  ))
}

# Keep only the two factions being compared
closeness_snapshots <- closeness_snapshots %>%
  filter(Faction %in% c("Grandi Curti", "Longhi"))

group_means <- closeness_snapshots %>%
  group_by(year, Faction) %>%
  summarise(mean_closeness = mean(closeness), mean_betweenness = mean(betweenness), .groups = "drop")

ggplot(closeness_snapshots, aes(x = closeness, fill = Faction, color = Faction)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.4, linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_rug(aes(color = Faction), sides = "b", linewidth = 0.6, alpha = 0.7, show.legend = FALSE) +
  geom_vline(data = group_means, aes(xintercept = mean_closeness, color = Faction),
             linetype = "dashed", linewidth = 0.8, show.legend = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~ year, ncol = 1) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("Grandi Curti" = "red", "Longhi" = "gold")) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Grandi Curti" = "red", "Longhi" = "gold")) +
  labs(x = "Closeness centrality", y = "Density") +
  theme_minimal()
@

Average values, however, provide only a partial picture. Since each faction comprises many families, it is also useful to examine the distribution of closeness centrality within each group. Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.closeness.histo} shows the density of closeness centrality for the \emph{longhi} and the \emph{grandi curti} in three representative years: \Sexpr{paste(years_of_interest, collapse=", ")}. 

In 1500, the two distributions are relatively compact and approximately bell-shaped, with the \emph{grandi curti} centered at slightly higher values of closeness than the \emph{longhi}. By \Sexpr{years_of_interest[2]}, when the \emph{longhi} had already overtaken the \emph{grandi curti} in average closeness, the picture has changed substantially. Both distributions become increasingly right-skewed and develop multiple peaks, indicating the emergence of subgroups of families with relatively high closeness and a more differentiated internal structure within each faction. 
By \Sexpr{years_of_interest[2]}, the average values have largely converged, with the \emph{longhi} retaining only a slight advantage. Although both factions retain a heterogeneous internal structure, their distributions become increasingly similar. This suggests that average closeness alone provides only a partial description of the structural differences between the two factions.

Taken together, the evidence presented in this section provides further insight into the research questions motivating this study. It suggests that the \emph{grandi curti} enjoyed a structural advantage until the second half of the sixteenth century, an advantage that may have facilitated the formation of the broad coalitions required to sustain their long-standing monopoly over the dogeship. At the same time, it points to a gradual convergence between the two principal factions around the turn of the seventeenth century.
Interestingly, this transition occurs at approximately the same time as the patrician network reaches its maximum size and subsequently begins to contract (Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.closeness}). Although this temporal coincidence does not establish a causal link, it suggests that changes in the size of the marriage pool may have contributed to the broader reorganization of the network.

\subsection{Network Reorganization}\label{ssec:reorganization}

To investigate whether the changes observed in proximity and centrality reflects a broader reorganization of the marriage network, Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.constraint} reports the evolution of three global network measures: edge density (magenta), average Burt's constraint (coral), and global transitivity (dark blue). Together, these measures capture complementary aspects of network organization and provide a more global perspective on the structural changes observed in the proximity measures.

<<casenuove.constraint, echo=F, fig.cap="Global evolution of the network in two structure-related aspects: average constraint and edge density", fig.height=3, fig.pos="h!tb">>=
# ggplot( transition_year, aes(x=year))+ geom_line( aes(y=global_transitivity, color="Global transitivity")) + 
#   geom_line( aes(y=edge_density, color="Edge density"))+
#   geom_line( aes(y=average_constraint, color="Average constraint")) + 
#   scale_color_manual( name=NULL,
#                       values = c("Global transitivity" = "darkblue", 
#                                  "Edge density" = "darkgreen",
#                                  "Average constraint" = "darkgray"))+
#   theme_minimal() + 
#   labs(y="Structural variable values")

ggplot(transition_year, aes(x = year)) +
  geom_line(aes(y = global_transitivity, color = "Global transitivity")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = edge_density, color = "Edge density")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = average_constraint, color = "Average constraint")) +
  scale_color_manual(
    name = NULL,
    values = c("Global transitivity" = "darkblue",
             "Edge density" = "magenta1",
             "Average constraint" = "coral"),
    # This argument forces the specific order you requested:
    limits = c("Global transitivity", "Average constraint", "Edge density")
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  labs(y = "Structural variable values")

min_constraint_year <- transition_year[which(transition_year$average_constraint == min(transition_year$average_constraint)),]$year
max_density_year <- transition_year[which(transition_year$edge_density == max(transition_year$edge_density)),]$year
max_transitivity_year <- transition_year[which(transition_year$global_transitivity == max(transition_year$global_transitivity)),]$year
@



Edge density, defined as the ratio between observed and possible marriage ties, increases gradually until approximately \Sexpr{max_density_year}, after which it begins to decline. On its own, however, this trend is difficult to interpret, because edge density depends jointly on the number of marriage ties and the size of the network. 

The second measure we consider is average Burt's constraint \citep{burt1992structural}, which is computed from the density of each family's ego network and measures the extent to which a family's marriage ties are concentrated within a tightly interconnected local neighborhood rather than connecting otherwise separate parts of the network. In Burt's terminology, low constraint is associated with brokerage across structural holes. As shown in the figure, average constraint declines throughout most of the period, reaching its minimum around \Sexpr{min_constraint_year}, before increasing slightly during the last years of the study period. At least part of its decline is likely to reflect the gradual disappearance of peripheral families with only one or very few marriage ties, whose constraint is necessarily high.

The behavior of the final measure, global transitivity, is more informative. It measures the tendency of marriages to form closed triangles and reaches its maximum around \Sexpr{max_transitivity_year}, well before edge density reaches its peak. Thus, although the network continues to become denser for some decades, new marriage ties contribute progressively less to local closure.  In other words, the observed pattern of marriages increasingly connected families that do not already share common neighbors, suggesting a gradual change in the organization of the marriage network. This interpretation is consistent with the changes observed in the proximity measures discussed above. As marriages increasingly connected previously separate parts of the network, the distinction between the two elite factions became progressively less pronounced, culminating in the crossover between inter- and intra-faction ASPL around \Sexpr{first_period} we observed in Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.analysis}.

Overall, these three measures suggest that the reorganization of the marriage network was gradual rather than abrupt. Although all three eventually reverse their trend, they do so at different times, indicating that different aspects of the network responded on different timescales. In particular, the decline in global transitivity suggests that marriages increasingly connected families belonging to different local neighborhoods. An important question is whether these new ties also crossed the traditional political divisions within the Venetian patriciate.

<<casenuove.assortativity, echo=F, message=F, fig.cap="Evolution of assortativity coefficients by factional affiliation.",  fig.pos="h!tb", fig.height=4>>=
ggplot( core_periphery_timeline, aes(x = year)) +
  geom_line(aes(y = assortativity_congiurati, color = "Grandi Curti")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = assortativity_longhi, color = "Longhi")) +
  geom_line(aes(y = assortativity_longhi_congiurati, color = "Inter Longhi - Grandi Curti")) +
  labs(x = "Year",
       y = "Assortativity") +
  scale_color_manual(name=NULL,
                     values = c("Grandi Curti" = "red", "Longhi" = "gold", "Inter Longhi - Grandi Curti" ="goldenrod")) +
  theme_minimal()

max_intra_curti_assortativity <- core_periphery_timeline[which(core_periphery_timeline$assortativity_congiurati == max(core_periphery_timeline$assortativity_congiurati)),]$year

#---- Compute correlation
library(zoo)

core_periphery_timeline <- core_periphery_timeline %>% arrange(year)

window_size <- 6  # 6 points x 5-year step = 30-year window

rolling_cor <- rollapply(
  data = core_periphery_timeline[, c("assortativity_congiurati", "assortativity_longhi")],
  width = window_size,
  FUN = function(x) cor(x[, 1], x[, 2]),
  by.column = FALSE,
  align = "center",
  fill = NA
)

rolling_cor_df <- data.frame(year = core_periphery_timeline$year, rolling_correlation = rolling_cor)

# ggplot(rolling_cor_df, aes(x = year, y = rolling_correlation)) +
#   geom_line() +
#   geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "grey50") +
#   labs(title = "30-year rolling correlation: Grandi Curti vs. Longhi assortativity",
#        x = "Year", y = "Rolling correlation") +
#   theme_minimal()

# Find the first year after which the rolling correlation stays positive for the rest of the series
rc <- rolling_cor_df$rolling_correlation
years <- rolling_cor_df$year

split_year <- NA
for (i in seq_along(rc)) {
  if (!is.na(rc[i]) && all(rc[i:length(rc)] > 0, na.rm = TRUE)) {
    split_year <- years[i]
    break
  }
}

rc_clean <- rolling_cor_df %>% filter(!is.na(rolling_correlation)) %>% arrange(year)

# Identify runs of consecutive same-sign values
rc_clean <- rc_clean %>%
  mutate(sign = sign(rolling_correlation),
         run_id = cumsum(sign != lag(sign, default = first(sign))))

# Find the longest run where sign is positive
positive_runs <- rc_clean %>%
  filter(sign > 0) %>%
  group_by(run_id) %>%
  summarise(start_year = min(year),
            end_year = max(year),
            length_years = end_year - start_year,
            n_points = n(),
            .groups = "drop") %>%
  arrange(desc(length_years))

longest_start <- positive_runs$start_year[1]
longest_end   <- positive_runs$end_year[1]

within_period <- core_periphery_timeline %>% filter(year >= longest_start & year <= longest_end)

cor_within <- cor.test(within_period$assortativity_congiurati, within_period$assortativity_longhi)

pre_period <- core_periphery_timeline %>% filter(year <= longest_start )

cor_pre <- cor.test(pre_period$assortativity_congiurati, pre_period$assortativity_longhi)


@

To address this question, Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.assortativity} reports the assortativity coefficients  \citep{newman2003mixing} with respect to factional affiliation. The intra-faction assortativity coefficients measure the tendency of the \emph{grandi curti} (red) and the \emph{longhi} (gold), respectively, to contract marriages within their own faction rather than with the rest of the patriciate. The \emph{longhi}--\emph{grandi curti}  coefficient (orange) measures whether, when marrying outside their own faction, the \emph{longhi} preferentially married members of the \emph{grandi curti} rather than other patrician families. For the intra-faction coefficients, positive values indicate a preference for marriages within the same faction, whereas for the \emph{longhi}--\emph{grandi curti} coefficient they indicate a preference for marriages between the two factions; negative values indicate the opposite tendency.

Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.assortativity} reveals distinct temporal patterns for the three assortativity coefficients. Although all three remain close to zero throughout the period, indicating relatively weak factional preferences in marriage choices, they evolve in markedly different ways. At the beginning of the period, the \emph{longhi} display the highest positive assortativity, whereas the \emph{grandi curti} show values close to zero or slightly negative. During the first half of the sixteenth century, however, the two trends move in opposite directions: assortativity among the \emph{grandi curti} increases steadily, reaching a peak around 1560, while that of the \emph{longhi} declines almost continuously. This visual impression is reflected in the correlation between the two assortativity series, which changes from \Sexpr{round(cor_pre$estimate,2)} before \Sexpr{longest_start} to \Sexpr{round(cor_within$estimate,2)} thereafter (both statistically significant), indicating that the two factions changed from following opposite assortativity trends to evolving in the same direction. The inter-faction coefficient follows a similar downward trajectory to that of the \emph{longhi}.
These trends suggest that, during the first half of the sixteenth century, the \emph{grandi curti} increasingly concentrated their marriages within their own political group, whereas the \emph{longhi} became progressively less endogamous. After the mid-sixteenth century, the assortativity of the \emph{longhi} falls below zero, indicating that marriages outside the faction became increasingly common. At the same time, the continued decline of the inter-faction coefficient suggests that these extra-faction marriages were progressively less concentrated on the \emph{grandi curti} alone and increasingly involved the wider patriciate.

This pattern is consistent with the convergence observed in the proximity measures discussed above. As marriages increasingly crossed traditional factional boundaries, the structural distinction between the two political groups became progressively less pronounced, contributing to the convergence in average path lengths and closeness centrality.

<<casenuove.number_of_marriages, echo=F, message=F, fig.cap="Temporal evolution of the different categories of marriages.", fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tb">>=
