max_marriages_extra_longhi <- year_at_max(number_of_marriages,"extra_longhi")
max_marriages_extra_grandi_curti <- year_at_max(number_of_marriages,"extra_congiurati")
max_marriages_intra_longhi <- year_at_max(number_of_marriages,"intra_longhi")
max_marriages_intra_grandi_curti <- year_at_max(number_of_marriages,"intra_congiurati")
max_marriages_inter <- year_at_max(number_of_marriages,"inter_congiurati_longhi")
@

To complement the structural measures discussed above, Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.number_of_marriages} reports the absolute number of marriages in each 75-year network. Unlike assortativity, which measures the relative tendency to contract marriages within or across factional boundaries, these counts describe the volume of marriage activity in the same intra-faction, extra-faction, and inter-faction categories introduced in the analysis of average shortest path length. Marriages between the \emph{longhi} and the rest of the patriciate are by far the most numerous, followed by those involving the \emph{grandi curti} and families outside their faction. Inter-faction marriages  occupy an intermediate position, whereas marriages within the \emph{longhi} and, especially, within the \emph{grandi curti} are less frequent. The series reach their maxima between \Sexpr{max_marriages_intra_grandi_curti} and \Sexpr{max_marriages_inter}. Thereafter, all five decline, although the contraction is considerably more pronounced for extra-faction marriages than for marriages within or between the two factions.

% These span a number of years at the end of which we have observed the peak in global transitivity (Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.assortativity}), as well as other peaks that also indicate structural changes: change of the sign of the correlation between {\em grandi curti} and {\em longhi} assortativity, the minimum of intra-{\em longhi} (\Sexpr{min_ASPL_longhi}) and extra-{\em longhi} ASPL (\Sexpr{min_ASPL_extra_longhi}), minimum average constraint (\Sexpr{min_constraint_year}) and maximum edge density (\Sexpr{max_density_year}).

These absolute counts provide a demographic context for the structural changes identified above. They show that the convergence in proximity and factional mixing developed during a prolonged expansion of marriage activity and continued as the overall number of marriages began to contract. The slightly different timing of the peaks also reinforces the conclusion that the reorganization of the network was gradual rather than concentrated around a single turning point.


<<casenuove.betweenness.evolution, echo=F, fig.cap = "Distribution of betweenness in two points in the evolution of the network. Solid line indicates median values peer faction.", fig.pos="h!tb">>=
group_stats <- closeness_snapshots %>%
  group_by(year, Faction) %>%
  summarise(
    mean_betweenness   = mean(betweenness, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_betweenness = median(betweenness, na.rm = TRUE),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

# Base density estimate, just to auto-scale how far below the axis labels sit
approx_max_density <- max(density(closeness_snapshots$betweenness, na.rm = TRUE)$y)
label_offset <- 0.35 * approx_max_density   # how far above/below the axis labels sit

top5_betweenness <- closeness_snapshots %>%
  group_by(year) %>%
  slice_max(betweenness, n = 5, with_ties = FALSE) %>%
  mutate(
    label_y = ifelse(Faction == "Longhi", -label_offset, label_offset),
    label_color = ifelse(Faction == "Longhi", "goldenrod", "red"),
    label_angle = ifelse(Faction == "Longhi", -90, 90)
  ) %>%
  ungroup()

years_to_keep <- c(1575, 1625)

closeness_snapshots_filtered <- closeness_snapshots %>% filter(year %in% years_to_keep)
group_stats_filtered         <- group_stats %>% filter(year %in% years_to_keep)
top5_betweenness_filtered    <- top5_betweenness %>% filter(year %in% years_to_keep)

ggplot(closeness_snapshots_filtered, aes(x = betweenness, fill = Faction, color = Faction)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.4, linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_rug(aes(color = Faction), sides = "b", linewidth = 0.6, alpha = 0.7, show.legend = FALSE) +
  geom_vline(data = group_stats_filtered, aes(xintercept = median_betweenness, color = Faction),
             linewidth = 0.8, show.legend = FALSE) +
  geom_segment(
    data = top5_betweenness_filtered,
    aes(x = betweenness, xend = betweenness, y = 0, yend = label_y * 0.3),
    color = top5_betweenness_filtered$label_color, linewidth = 0.3, alpha = 0.6
  ) +
  geom_text(
    data = top5_betweenness_filtered,
    aes(x = betweenness, y = label_y, label = family, angle = label_angle),
    color = top5_betweenness_filtered$label_color,
    size = 3, fontface = "bold", hjust = 0.5, vjust = 0.5
  ) +
  facet_wrap(~ year, ncol = 1) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("Grandi Curti" = "red", "Longhi" = "gold")) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Grandi Curti" = "red", "Longhi" = "gold")) +
  labs(x = "Betweenness centrality", y = "Density",
       title = "Distribution of betweenness centrality by faction") +
  coord_cartesian(clip = "off") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(plot.margin = margin(30, 10, 60, 10),
        legend.position = "none")
@

\subsection{Brokerage and Political Influence}\label{ssec:brokerage}


The preceding analyses have shown that the structural advantage initially enjoyed by the \emph{grandi curti} gradually diminished as the marriage network became more cohesive and the distinction between the two principal factions weakened. This raises a final question: once this faction-level advantage had largely disappeared, did the network begin to favor a different type of structural position? To address this issue,  Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.betweenness.evolution} compares the distribution of betweenness centrality by faction in two representative network snapshots: \Sexpr{years_to_keep[1]}, during the transition identified in the previous sections, and \Sexpr{years_to_keep[2]}, after the main structural changes had taken place. The vertical lines indicate the median betweenness of each faction, while the labels identify the five families with the highest betweenness in each period. Those above the axis belong to the \emph{grandi curti} and those below it to the \emph{longhi}, using the corresponding faction colours (red and gold).

The two distributions display a substantial overlap in both periods; their median values nevertheless diverge over time, with the \emph{longhi} exceeding the \emph{grandi curti} by a wider margin in \Sexpr{years_to_keep[2]} than in \Sexpr{years_to_keep[1]}, indicating that brokerage positions became relatively more common among the former. The figure further shows that the families occupying the highest betweenness positions belong to both factions. It also reveals considerable turnover among the principal brokers: only Donato and Zorzi remain among the leading families across the two snapshots, while most of the highest-betweenness positions are occupied by different families fifty years later.

These results suggest that the structural consequences of the network reorganization extended beyond changes in the relationship between the two factions. During the fifteenth and much of the sixteenth century, the structural advantage of the \emph{grandi curti} appears to have derived primarily from the cohesion of the faction as a whole. By the early seventeenth century, brokerage positions occupied by a relatively small number of highly connected families from both factions became increasingly prominent. Rather than reflecting a transfer of influence from one faction to the other, these findings are consistent with a shift in the level at which structural advantage operated—from political factions as collective actors to strategically positioned individual families.

%This period included the return of the {\em longhi} to the office of doge; Marcantonio Memmo was elected in 1612, traditionally maked as the end of the {\em ducali} run. As representative of the {\em longhi}, it is an unusual election: the betweenness in this network snapshot is \Sexpr{round(closeness_snapshots[closeness_snapshots$family == "Memmo" & closeness_snapshots$year == 1625,]$betweenness,2)}, way below the median and placing the family among the lower-betweenness cluster at the lowest end of the axis. If it is remarkable, it is only because it is not part of the {\em grandi curti family}. However, from this analysis emerges other election that marks the regime change: that of Leonardo Donato in 1606. This family had a \Sexpr{round(closeness_snapshots[closeness_snapshots$family == "Donato" & closeness_snapshots$year == 1625,]$betweenness,2)} betweenness in the \Sexpr{DEPTH_IN_YEARS} network ending in 1625, and in fact it is the one with the second-highest betweenness, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:casenuove.betweenness.evolution} (lower, but in fact this family has the highest {\em grandi curti} betweenness in the other period examined too).

This interpretation is also consistent with the sequence of dogal elections following the end of the \emph{grandi curti} monopoly. Several of the doges elected after 1612—--including members of the Bembo, Donato, Priuli, Contarini, and Corner families--—belonged to families occupying some of the most prominent brokerage positions in the marriage network. Significantly, these families came from both the \emph{grandi curti} and the \emph{longhi}, suggesting that brokerage positions had become more relevant than factional affiliation alone.


\section{Conclusions and discussion}

This study examined the evolution of the Venetian patrician marriage network over the two centuries of the \emph{grandi curti}'s monopoly of the dogeship and the years immediately following its end. Rather than identifying a single structural break, the analysis reveals a gradual reorganization of the network, reflected in different ways by the various network measures.

One methodological point deserves emphasis. Marriage networks evolve more gradually than political institutions because marriages depend not only on strategic choices but also on demographic opportunities, including the contemporaneous availability of suitable spouses across the patriciate. Consequently, structural changes should not be expected to coincide precisely with political events. The end of the \emph{grandi curti}'s monopoly in 1612 therefore marks only one stage in a broader reorganization of the marriage network that began earlier and continued well beyond that date.

A first finding concerns the existence of a structural advantage enjoyed by the \emph{grandi curti}. Multiple measures of network proximity consistently indicate that, until the second half of the sixteenth century, these families occupied more favourable positions within the marriage network than the \emph{longhi}. This advantage is particularly evident in closeness centrality, which captures each family's overall position within the network, and is consistent with the \emph{grandi curti}'s prolonged monopoly over the dogeship. During the same period, the increasing intra-faction assortativity of the \emph{grandi curti} suggests that marriages were progressively concentrated within the faction. Although this pattern does not by itself demonstrate a deliberate political strategy, it is consistent with the interpretation that marriage alliances contributed to reinforcing cohesion within the ruling coalition.
FORSE QUESTA SECONDA FRASE NON E' NECESSARIA


A second finding concerns the gradual disappearance of this advantage. The convergence observed in average path lengths, closeness centrality, assortativity, and the global structural measures suggests that the distinction between the two principal factions progressively weakened during the late sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries. Importantly, these measures did not change simultaneously. Instead, different aspects of the marriage network evolved on different timescales, indicating that the reorganization was progressive rather than concentrated around a single turning point.

A final finding concerns the role of brokerage in the marriage network. Before 1612, the families elected as doges were not necessarily those occupying the most prominent brokerage positions.  This suggests that political success depended primarily on the structural advantage enjoyed by the \emph{grandi curti} as a coalition rather than on the network position of individual families. By contrast, following the end of the monopoly, doges increasingly came from families occupying prominent brokerage positions, irrespective of factional affiliation.

More broadly, these findings illustrate the value of longitudinal network analysis for the study of historical political systems. In contexts such as Renaissance Venice, where direct evidence of political alliances is scarce, marriage networks provide an indirect but informative source for reconstructing patterns of political cohesion and competition. 

